```

## Tutorials

Print which elements appear more than once:

Explanation:We go through the array one element at a time.- The outer loop picks a number (like the first1).- The inner loop compares it with all the numbers that come after it.- If a match is found, we print it as a duplicate.In this example, the program finds2twice and1twice, so it prints them as duplicates.

### Code Examples

```
Example
int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 5, 1};

for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
  for (int j = i + 1; j < nums.length; j++) {
    if (nums[i] == nums[j]) {
      System.out.println("Duplicate: " + nums[i]);
    }
  }
}
Try it Yourself »
```

## Tutorials

Randomly change the order of elements in an array usingCollections.shuffle():

### Code Examples

```
Example
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    String[] fruits = {"Apple", "Banana", "Cherry", "Mango"};
    List<String> list = Arrays.asList(fruits);

    Collections.shuffle(list);

    System.out.println(list);
  }
}
